# PowerPC 604e — the test programs in test/ that apply to this core, one per line.
# Blank lines and #-comments are ignored.  See the note above TEST_SRCS_ALL in
# the Makefile for how this is used, and test/p604e/ for the expected outputs.
#
# With four exceptions, every program listed here was checked against the
# 601's expected file and differs from it ONLY in bit 19 of the MSR images it
# captures — MSR[ME], which the 601 sets at reset (MPC601UM Table 2-11) and
# this core leaves clear.  So those are not fresh snapshots of current
# behaviour: they are the 601's hand-derived expectations, re-derived through
# one documented difference.  The programs' own headers derive their values
# from the manual and remain the reason to believe them.
#
# The four exceptions — alignppc32, alignres, frspnan and fpscrsoft — have
# per-core expected files derived in their own headers: alignment and the
# VXSOFT/VXSQRT/NI bits genuinely differ between the 601 and this core, and
# frspnan pins a §D.4.1 case no older program covered.
#
# EXCLUDED, and why.  Each of these needs a test written for this core rather
# than a rerun of the 601's, and none is excluded because it fails for an
# unexplained reason:
#
#   align,        both encode the 601's boundary rules — the blanket 256 MB
#   alignmulti    crossing fault (§5.4.6.1.1) and the misaligned-multiple
#                 POWER compatibility that goes with it — which this core
#                 does not have (§4.5.6: no boundary rule for elementary
#                 accesses, and a misaligned lmw/stmw faults outright).
#                 alignppc32 and alignres cover this core's rules instead.
#   bat, dcbz     both set up a 601 BAT — one unified array, keyed Ks/Ku, with
#                 the valid bit in the lower register and block sizes to 8 MB.
#                 This core has SPLIT IBAT/DBAT arrays keyed Vs/Vp with the
#                 valid bits in the upper register (cores/common/ppc32_mmu.sail),
#                 so the same SPR writes describe something else entirely.
#                 dcbz depends on a BAT to make a block write-through.
#   clcs, lscbx,  the POWER holdover instructions (cores/p601/p601_insts_power.sail).
#   power*        601-only, and correctly illegal here.
#   dec           the 601's decrementer is 25 bits wide and steps once per
#                 128 ns RTC tick; this core's is 32 bits and steps with the
#                 time base (cores/common/ppc32_regs.sail).
#   iabr          the 601 reports an IABR match as a run-mode exception at
#                 0x02000 under HID1's mode field; this core has a dedicated
#                 instruction address breakpoint exception at 0x01300 and a
#                 differently shaped IABR.
#   lendian       little-endian mode is HID0[LM] on the 601 and MSR[LE] here.
#   msr           exercises exactly the set of MSR bits the 601 implements.
#   trace         the 601 vectors MSR[SE] tracing to the run-mode vector
#                 0x02000; this core uses the architected 0x00D00.
#   ioseg         the 601's I/O controller interface segments.  This core does
#                 still implement the direct-store interface (MPC604UM §5.5),
#                 but a data transaction on it needs a bus this model does not
#                 have, so the program dies rather than producing a state to
#                 compare (cores/p604/p604_core.sail).

alignppc32
alignres
arith
branch
branchreg
bytereverse
cacheops
compare
crlogic
crmove
dabr
extctl
fparith
fpconv
fpexc
fpmadd
fpmem
fpmemx
fpscr
fpscrsoft
fpsingle
frspnan
illegal
load
logical
mcrf
muldiv
multiple
pagetable
reserve
rotshift
segreg
smoke
store
string
sync
syscall
trap

# Written for these cores rather than inherited from the 601: each exercises a
# facility the 601 does not have, and none is in test/p601.tests.
timebase
ppc32bat
fpopt
